WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.000
Meet the seed twin

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:10.000
Water uptake

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:13.000
The seed is cut away so you can see inside: seed coat (brown), endosperm (pale), and the embryo coloured by tissue.

00:00:13.167 --> 00:00:18.667
Water floods in. By 12 h the seed holds 0.81 g of water per gram of dry seed, and its mucilage coat swells.

00:00:19.333 --> 00:00:22.333
Two hormones decide

00:00:20.833 --> 00:00:25.833
Left: abscisic acid, which holds the seed dormant. Right: gibberellin, which drives germination.

00:00:26.333 --> 00:00:32.333
Once wet, the embryo breaks ABA down: by 12 h it has fallen from 10.97 to 3.29, while gibberellin builds up from the radicle tip.

00:00:37.333 --> 00:00:40.333
The expansion wave

00:00:39.048 --> 00:00:45.048
Cells begin to expand in the lower hypocotyl and the expansion spreads along the axis: the pattern Bassel and colleagues measured cell by cell in 3D.

00:00:46.905 --> 00:00:53.905
The seed coat splits at 24.0 h, and the radicle pushes through the endosperm at 32.0 h. That is germination.

00:00:56.033 --> 00:00:59.033
Oxygen inside the seed

00:00:57.867 --> 00:01:05.867
Respiring tissue uses oxygen faster than it can diffuse in. At 34 h the seed surface sits at 13.8 kPa but the core of the axis falls to 0.00 kPa.

00:01:09.033 --> 00:01:12.033
The seedling

00:01:10.283 --> 00:01:16.283
Root hairs anchor the seedling at 38.0 h; the root turns down with gravity and the hypocotyl lifts the cotyledons out.

00:01:18.033 --> 00:01:24.033
The cotyledons leave the seed coat at 65.3 h and, in the light, open at 73.0 h.

00:01:26.533 --> 00:01:32.533
By 120 h the root is 3.4 mm long and the hypocotyl 2.3 mm.
